Index: doc/manual/rw-FAQ.texi
===================================================================
--- doc/manual/rw-FAQ.texi	(revision 90763)
+++ doc/manual/rw-FAQ.texi	(working copy)
@@ -1761,6 +1761,26 @@
 
 With a native build of R on ARM, exclude @file{\x64} from the above.
 
+R built with @I{FlexiBLAS} (@code{USE_FLEXIBLAS = YES} in
+@file{src/gnuwin32/MkRules.local}) can switch the BLAS and LAPACK
+at run time instead: set the environment variable @env{FLEXIBLAS},
+for example to @code{OPENBLAS-OPENMP}, before starting R, or change the
+default in @file{R_HOME\etc\flexiblasrc}.
+
+The two @I{OpenBLAS} backends differ in their threads.  With no variable
+set, both use every core, up to 64.  @code{OPENBLAS-OPENMP}, the
+@I{OpenBLAS} of @I{Rtools}, takes its count from @env{OMP_NUM_THREADS},
+capped by @env{OMP_THREAD_LIMIT}, and ignores @env{OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS}.
+It has its own copy of the @I{OpenMP} run time, so it does not see the
+parallel region of a package that calls the BLAS from several @I{OpenMP}
+threads: such calls run one at a time, and each calling thread keeps a
+team of threads of its own.  @code{OPENBLAS-THREADS} keeps one pool of
+threads for all callers and takes its count from
+@env{OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS} or @env{OMP_NUM_THREADS}, but not from
+@env{OMP_THREAD_LIMIT}.  With code that runs threads of its own, one BLAS
+thread avoids running more threads than cores.  On x86_64 @I{OpenBLAS}
+commits 128MB of memory per thread and keeps it until R exits.
+
